r10 sends OSPF Hello packets with the **E-bit cleared** (stub area behaviour), while r2 sends Hellos with the **E-bit set** (non-stub/normal area). OSPF requires the Options field — including the E-bit — to match between neighbours before allowing the adjacency to progress past `ExStart`. The mismatch causes both sides to discard each other's Hellos, leaving `nbr=0` on both eth2 (r2) and eth0 (r10). With no ABR adjacency, r10 has no way to receive inter-area LSAs, so every route beyond area 4 purges from its RIB.

